#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Linux stand-in for Apple's momc (Core Data model compiler), for SwiftBuild under xtool.

SwiftBuild contract (swift-build Sources/SWBApplePlatform, Specs/CoreData.xcspec +
CoreDataCompiler.swift):
  compile:  momc [--sdkroot P] [--iphoneos-deployment-target V] [--module M]
            [--no-warnings ...] InputFile ProductResourcesDir/
            -> writes ProductResourcesDir/<base>.momd (versioned .xcdatamodeld: one
               <version>.mom per version + VersionInfo.plist) or <base>.mom (bare
               .xcdatamodel). The destination directory must already exist.
  codegen:  momc [--dry-run] --action generate [--swift-version V] [options] InputFile
            DERIVED_FILE_DIR/CoreDataGenerated/<ModelName>
            --dry-run prints the absolute paths of the files it would generate, one per line,
            and nothing at all when no entity opts into code generation; --action generate
            writes those files (entities with codeGenerationType=class get
            <Entity>+CoreDataClass.swift and +CoreDataProperties.swift, category gets only the
            properties file, plus one <Model>+CoreDataModel.swift when anything is generated).
Output matches Xcode 27.0 momc (tool version 25249): NSKeyedArchiver binary-plist .mom per
version with the same keys, VersionInfo.plist, `note: Model <v> version checksum:` lines on
stderr, `path:Entity.prop: warning: ... [2]` diagnostics, and Xcode 27's generated Swift text.
Verified against Apple's momc on macOS: model loader comparison, store round trip.
"""
